. replace unpaidwork =24 if unpaidwork>24
(10 real changes made)

. 
. gen ln_unpaidwork = ln(unpaidwork)
(22 missing values generated)

. 
. 
. ** Independent variable:
. ***** First born child is daughter
. gen d_daughter=1 if kidsex01==2 // 1 if the first-born child is female
(246 missing values generated)

. replace d_daughter=0 if kidsex01==1 // 0 if the first-born child is m
(246 real changes made)

. 
. 
. ** Control variables: 
. *** male dummy
. gen male=1 if sex ==1
(243 missing values generated)

. replace male =0 if sex ==2
(243 real changes made)

. 
. *** age --> see above
. 
. *** number of other daughters
. gen num_daughters_02_05=0

. replace num_daughters_02_05= num_daughters_02_05+1 if kidsex02==2
(166 real changes made)

. replace num_daughters_02_05= num_daughters_02_05+1 if kidsex03==2
(62 real changes made)

. replace num_daughters_02_05= num_daughters_02_05+1 if kidsex04==2
(14 real changes made)

. replace num_daughters_02_05= num_daughters_02_05+1 if kidsex05==2
(1 real change made)

. 
. *** number of children --> see above 
. 
. *** race of the respondent (dummy equals one if White-Caucasian vs. zero if n
> ot)
. * not included in year 2019
. 
. *** age of the first-born child at the point of the survey
. gen age_kid_01= year - kidgeb01 if kidgeb01>0
(1 missing value generated)

. 
. *** highest educational level achieved by the respondent (linear value, 0 = w
> ithout school degree, 1 = school degree, 2 = professional degree (Ausbildung)
> , 3 = university degree)
. * not available. Instead number of years in education
. gen education_yrs= pgbilzeit if pgbilzeit>=0
(6 missing values generated)

. 
. *** marital status (dummy, equals one if married or living with a partner, ze
> ro otherwise)
. * we control only for married, as living in together (not-marries) is not ava
> ilable.
. gen married=1 if pgfamstd==1
(124 missing values generated)

. replace married =0 if pgfamstd!=1
(124 real changes made)

. 
. *** political party affiliation
. * will reduce the sample by half
. 
. *** religion
. * not included in year 2019
. 
. *** employment status (linear value, 0 not employed, 1 part-time, 2 full-time
> , 3 self-employed)
. gen employment = 0 if pgemplst ==5
(372 missing values generated)

. replace employment = 1 if pgemplst ==2 | pgemplst ==4
(137 real changes made)

. replace employment = 3 if pgemplst ==1
(233 real changes made)

. 
. *** income
. gen income = pglabgro if pglabgro>=0
(62 missing values generated)

. * The missing values are set to 0 for those respondents who chose "doesn't ap
> ply" and are not employed
. replace income =0 if pglabgro ==-2 & employment ==0
(62 real changes made)

. 
. *** east/west Germany (dummy)
. gen west=1 if sampreg  ==1
(91 missing values generated)

. replace west=0 if sampreg==2
(91 real changes made)

. 
. *** interaction male and d_daughert:
. gen maleXd_daughter = male * d_daughter

. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
.         * We preregistered two separate regressions for males and females. Ho
> wever, we can't run two regressions based on the rules of the project. That's
>  why, we now run one regression with an interaction term of male and daugter 
> (as a first child) and report this coefficient. 
. 
. 
.         * We use the reported number of hours spend for housholdwork, errands
>  and childcare (pli0043_h, pli0040, pli0044_h) to compute the number of hours
>  spent for unpaid work. We assume, that the equality in the houshold measures
>  the distribution of the unpaid work in the household. If having a daugher le
> ads to greater equality, men who have a daugher as a first child should spend
>  more hours fpor unpaid work as compared to women.  
. 
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear OLS regression analysis with the varia
> ble ln_unpaidwork as dependent variable and the dummy variable d_daughter and
>  male, and their interaction as independent variable
.         
.         * Following the pre-registered idea of heterogeneous effects by gende
> r, our main variabel of interest is the interaction term maleXd_daughter.
. 
. reg ln_unpaidwork d_daughter male maleXd_daughter age num_daughters_02_05 num
> ber_kids age_kid_01 education_yrs married income employment west, ro

